Load rendering throttle, done safely: coalesce inside the RunLoopObserver.

Re-implements the load-time paint-storm coalescing that builds 465-467
shipped and build 468 reverted, WITHOUT the freeze regression.

Why the old attempts froze: parts 2/3 intercepted
LayerTreeHost::scheduleRenderingUpdate() and, while throttled, returned
early *without scheduling* the RenderingUpdate RunLoopObserver, standing
in a one-shot Timer to re-drive it later. But that observer is
Type::Repeating on purpose: once scheduled it fires every main-loop
iteration and renderingUpdateRunLoopObserverFired() simply returns while
m_isWaitingForRenderer blocks it, so a pending update can NEVER be lost.
Replacing that self-healing poll with a one-shot timer dropped updates
whenever the compositor was mid-flight -> permanent rendering freeze
(deterministic repro: swipe while reddit.com loads).

The safe enforcement point is the observer callback itself. Leave the
scheduling path completely stock (the repeating observer keeps polling),
and in renderingUpdateRunLoopObserverFired() defer the *work* -- return
early WITHOUT invalidating the observer -- until
WEBKIT_LOAD_RENDERING_INTERVAL_MS has elapsed since the last
updateRendering(), while the ProgressTracker main-load window
(Page::isRenderingThrottledForLoad(), from webkit-load-rendering-throttle-env.patch)
is open. Because the observer stays scheduled it re-fires next iteration
and retries on its own; m_isWaitingForRenderer and the composition
handshake are untouched. Dirty regions accumulate between updates, so the
batched update paints the union once instead of once per landing
resource (~340 Mpx -> ~35-50% fewer painted px per franceinfo load,
device-measured under the old throttle).

Safety cap: a single load never stays throttled longer than
loadRenderingThrottleMaxDuration (10s) -- reddit/onche hold the progress
bar open indefinitely, and after the cap we fall back to stock full-rate
updates for the rest of that load. Applies ON TOP of
webkit-load-rendering-throttle-env.patch. Unset/0 = off (stock).

